WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.360
Netflix is set to unleash a wave of exciting
new content throughout the month of September.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.680
Stuck on what to stream?

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.520
Here are the best movies and TV shows to watch
on Netflix in September 2020, plus everything

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.060
to see before it's gone for good.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.660
In September 2020, Netflix subscribers can
go back to the Back to the Future film franchise.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.580
The streamer introduced the full trilogy to
its digital shelves in May 2020, then removed

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.820
them in July 2020.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.320
But just a couple of months later, Marty McFly
and Doc Brown will return to Netflix in their

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.060
complete trilogy glory.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.860
Anyone who missed out over the summer can
stream all three movies beginning on September

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:35.580
1.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.260
The sci-fi action fun doesn't end there, though.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.379
Also headed to Netflix next month is Freaks,
You're One of Us, the action-packed, German-language

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:48.870
superhero film that follows working mom Wendy
as she learns she possesses powers of invulnerability

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.640
and super-strength, which were previously
suppressed by medication.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.380
Wendy teams up with two other powered people
to seek out others just like them.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.940
But their search soon grows complicated as
they end up uncovering a conspiracy bigger

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:03.889
than they ever thought could exist.

00:01:03.889 --> 00:01:07.390
Freaks, You're One of Us hits Netflix on September
2.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.780
Later in the month, catch the 2011 sci-fi
sports movie Real Steel, starring Hugh Jackman

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:18.049
as a former boxer whose life changes in major
ways after robots come to replace human combatants

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:18.860
in the sport.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.280
Struggling to make end's meet by building
robots out of scrap metal, Charlie reunites

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.439
with his estranged son Max, and together,
they exact a plan that will put their names

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.140
on the map: create a bot that can become a
champion.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.320
Real Steel will drop on Netflix on September
24.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.700
In September 2020, Netflix is intent on making
sure that we all get the lion's share of laughs.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.940
The first of the month alone gives us a few
modern classics, including 2002's hilarious

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:51.460
and heartfelt Barbershop, 2011's strip-tastic
Magic Mike, and the 2008 buddy stoner comedy

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.880
Pineapple Express.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.840
There are also some more retro offerings on
the way for those who want to tickle their

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.820
funny bones on the way down Memory Lane.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.920
There's 1993's Coneheads, which stars Dan
Aykroyd and Jane Curtin as the heads of the

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.400
clueless alien family they popularized on
Saturday Night Live, and 1978's Grease, a

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.480
timeless tale worthy of at least a dozen more
rewatches.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.980
Other comedy selections include the 2005 film
adaptation of the Broadway play The Producers

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.569
with Matthew Broderick and Nathan Lane, which
hits Netflix on September 1; How to Train

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:27.480
Your Dragon 2, which arrives on September
11; and 2005's Waiting...which is required

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.420
viewing for anyone who has ever worked in
a service industry, popping up on Netflix

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:32.820
on September 23.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:38.420
There's also the Cameron Diaz-starring Bad
Teacher, the 2011 feature about a…well,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.180
subpar educator that drops on September 27.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.660
"Alright.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:43.640
Let's do this."

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.820
Those wanting even more laughs can stream
a quartet of new standup specials on Netflix

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.060
in September 2020.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.060
Last Comic Standing season 7 winner Felipe
Esparza, whose previous specials They're Not

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:59.730
Gonna Laugh At You and Translate This aired
on Showtime and HBO, respectively, will make

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.820
his Netflix debut with Bad Decisions on September
1.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.480
Then, on September 3, Brazilian comic Afonso
Padilha brings his debut set Classless to

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.420
the streamer.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.200
Veteran British comic Michael McIntyre takes
over the spotlight on the 15th with a brand-new

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.460
batch of material entitled Showman.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.720
And on the 29th, podcaster, actress, and The
Circle host Michelle Buteau unleashes her

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.260
new special, the aptly-titled Welcome to Buteaupia.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.580
While there aren't a ton of new drama selections
heading to Netflix in September, the few that

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.260
we will see are worth watching.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:37.800
There's 2018's Adrift, which stars Shailene
Woodley and Sam Claflin as a couple who must

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.230
navigate the treacherous waters of the Pacific
in a tiny, damaged boat after their yacht

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.730
is destroyed by a hurricane.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:49.400
Then, 1989's Glory, an acclaimed Civil War
drama with a killer cast that includes Matthew

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.500
Broderick, Denzel Washington, Morgan Freeman,
and Andre Braugher.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:59.300
And finally, 2018's Wildlife, Paul Dano's
directorial debut that follows the travails

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.060
of a troubled couple portrayed by Jake Gyllenhaal
and Carey Mulligan.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.680
All three movies hit Netflix on September
1.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.239
Sitting just beyond the realm of drama in
the mystery genre is one of the most highly-anticipated

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:15.800
films of late 2020: Enola Holmes, based on
Nancy Springer's Enola Holmes Mysteries series

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:16.780
of novels.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:22.079
The flick stars Stranger Things' Millie Bobby
Brown in the title role, supported by a fantastic

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:27.380
cast that includes everybody's favorite British
super-hunk Henry Cavill as Enola's older brother

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:32.860
Sherlock; Sam Claflin (again) as her other
older brother Mycroft; and Helena Bonham Carter

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.920
as the trio's mother, Eudoria.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:40.540
Warner Bros. originally had Enola Holmes set
up for a theatrical release, but Netflix acquired

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.880
the movie in April, and will release it on
September 23.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.540
For horror fans on Netflix, there are some
cool new selections to cue up starting on

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.691
September 1, including the extraordinary 1998
B-movie Anaconda and the 2002 Silence of the

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.580
Lambs prequel Red Dragon.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.860
But the ball really gets rolling on September
4 with I'm Thinking of Ending Things, the

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.520
new psychological horror film from Charlie
Kaufman.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.319
The flick stars Jessie Buckley and Jesse Plemons
as a couple whose trip to meet the young man's

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.200
parents goes horribly awry.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.620
It's hard to say what terror might ensue purely
based on the fact that the script for I'm

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:18.780
Thinking of Ending Things comes from Kaufman,
a truly original screenwriter who provided

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.610
the world with the cinematic mind-screws Being
John Malkovich, Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.780
Mind, and Synecdoche, New York.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:31.620
September 10 on Netflix brings us The Babysitter:
Killer Queen, the sequel to director McG's

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.460
obscenely enjoyable 2017 horror-comedy The
Babysitter.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.740
Finally, on September 16, subscribers can
stream the hotly anticipated film The Devil

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.690
All the Time, the adaptation of Donald Ray
Pollock's 2011 Southern Gothic thriller novel

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.229
of the same name, with a cast that includes
Marvel Cinematic Universe alums Tom Holland

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:51.440
and Sebastian Stan.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.220
"Excuse me preacher, you got time for a sinner?"

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.020
September 2020 will also bring plenty of good
stuff for TV binge-watchers.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:05.200
All eight seasons of the underrated CW sitcom
Girlfriends is a pretty decent haul in and

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.340
of itself, and it'll drop on Netflix on September
11.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.259
Fans of The Curse of Oak Island and Van Helsing
will finally get to binge the fourth seasons

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.400
of those shows on September 15 and September
27, respectively.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:23.060
As far as Netflix original series go, hold
onto your butts: The first season of the amazing-looking

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:28.349
animated series Jurassic World: Camp Cretaceous
and the first season of the One Flew Over

00:06:28.349 --> 00:06:33.440
the Cuckoo's Nest prequel Ratched, starring
Sarah Paulson, both drop on September 18.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.560
And heads up, foodies: The second season of
Jon Favreau and chef Roy Choi's The Chef Show

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.980
debuts on Netflix on September 24.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.820
If you're one of those poor souls who missed
the fourth and final season of the widely

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.750
acclaimed NBC sitcom The Good Place, you can
finally remedy that unfortunate situation

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:51.780
on September 26.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.380
"It's either this, or start Bloodline, and,
I don't know, I just don't feel like I can

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.600
see Kyle Chandler as anyone else but Coach
Taylor."

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.260
While many great movies and TV series are
arriving on Netflix in September 2020, the

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.740
streamer is still doing some housekeeping
and taking some options off the table.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:13.520
In the first half of the month, subscribers
will say goodbye to all seven seasons of ABC's

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.090
fantasy drama Once Upon a Time, the first
season of Cold Case Files, the 2004 sci-fi

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:22.080
psychological horror-thriller The Forgotten,
and more.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.140
The back half of September 2020 will see even
more removals: the South Korean action-horror

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:32.169
Train to Busan, the first and third Jurassic
Park movies, the underrated Heath Ledger flick

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:37.020
A Knight's Tale , and director David Fincher's
Oscar-winning The Social Network, a modern

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.720
classic movie which will celebrate its 10th
anniversary on October 1st, just a day after

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:42.780
it leaves Netflix.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:44.440
"But you gotta come back.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.020
Somewhere around the end of November, or early
December.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.460
It's gonna be outta control, you gotta come
back for it."

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:51.740
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:55.820
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies and TV shows are coming soon.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.480
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

